    description:
      'One of the most influential works in Western thought. Socrates explores the nature of justice, the ideal state, and the Allegory of the Cave.',
    publishedYear: -375,
    totalCopies: 4,
    fileName: 'the-republic.html',
    htmlContent:
      '<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang="en">\n<head>\n  <meta charset="UTF-8" />\n  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />\n  <title>The Republic — Plato</title>\n  <style>\n    :root { --bg: #f7f5f0; --fg: #2a2520; --accent: #3d6b8e; --muted: #6b7f8a; }\n    @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { :root { --bg: #141820; --fg: #cdd4de; --accent: #6ba3d6; --muted: #7a8ea0; } }\n    * { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }\n    body { font-family: \'Georgia\', serif; background: var(--bg); color: var(--fg); line-height: 1.8; max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2rem 1.5rem; }\n    .header { text-align: center; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--accent); padding-bottom: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 2rem; }\n    h1 { font-size: 2.2rem; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 0.25rem; }\n    .author { font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--muted); font-style: italic; }\n    .meta { display: flex; justify-content: center; gap: 1.5rem; margin-top: 0.75rem; font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--muted); }\n    h2 { font-size: 1.4rem; color: var(--accent); margin: 2rem 0 0.75rem; }\n    p { margin-bottom: 1rem; text-align: justify; }\n    blockquote { border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); padding: 0.75rem 1rem; margin: 1.5rem 0; font-style: italic; color: var(--muted); background: rgba(61,107,142,0.05); }\n    .dialogue { background: rgba(61,107,142,0.04); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 1.5rem 0; }\n    .dialogue-title { font-size: 0.9rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 2px; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }\n    .speaker { font-weight: bold; color: var(--accent); }\n    .footer { text-align: center; margin-top: 3rem; padding-top: 1rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--accent); font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--muted); }\n  </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <div class="header">\n    <h1>The Republic</h1>\n    <p class="author">by Plato</p>\n    <div class="meta">\n      <span>Written: c. 375 BC</span>\n      <span>Genre: Philosophy</span>\n      <span>10 Books</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2>About This Book</h2>\n  <p>Plato\'s <em>Republic</em> is one of the most influential works in the history of Western thought. Written as a Socratic dialogue, it asks a deceptively simple question: <em>What is justice?</em> In pursuing the answer, Socrates and his interlocutors construct an ideal city-state, explore the nature of the soul, debate the role of art, and present the famous Allegory of the Cave — a parable about enlightenment that resonates to this day.</p>\n\n  <blockquote>"The myth of the cave teaches us that we must question the shadows we take for reality."</blockquote>\n\n  <h2>Overview</h2>\n  <p>The dialogue begins when Socrates is challenged to prove that justice is intrinsically valuable — not just useful for its rewards. To answer, he proposes building a city "in speech," arguing that justice in the state mirrors justice in the individual soul.</p>\n  <p>His ideal city is divided into three classes: producers (farmers, artisans), guardians (warriors), and philosopher-kings — rulers who have ascended from the cave of ignorance to behold the Form of the Good. Justice, Socrates argues, consists in each class (and each part of the soul) performing its proper function.</p>\n  <p>The <em>Republic</em> also contains Plato\'s theory of Forms — the idea that the physical world is merely a shadow of a higher, perfect reality. Education, for Plato, is not filling an empty vessel but turning the soul toward the light.</p>\n\n  <div class="dialogue">\n    <div class="dialogue-title">The Allegory of the Cave — Book VII</div>\n    <p><span class="speaker">Socrates:</span> Imagine human beings living in an underground cave, which has a mouth open towards the light. They have been there from childhood, with their legs and necks chained so that they cannot move, and can only see before them.</p>\n    <p>Above and behind them a fire is blazing, and between the fire and the prisoners there is a raised way, along which a low wall has been built, like the screen which puppet players have in front of them. Behind this wall, people carry all sorts of vessels and statues, which appear over the wall.</p>\n    <p><span class="speaker">Glaucon:</span> You have shown me a strange image, and they are strange prisoners.</p>\n    <p><span class="speaker">Socrates:</span> Like ourselves. They see only their own shadows, or the shadows of one another, which the fire throws on the opposite wall of the cave.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2>Key Themes</h2>\n  <p><strong>Justice:</strong> The central question. Plato argues that justice is harmony — each part doing its job — in both the city and the soul. An unjust person is internally divided; a just person is whole.</p>\n  <p><strong>Knowledge vs. Opinion:</strong> The divided line and the cave allegory distinguish between those who merely believe (prisoners watching shadows) and those who truly know (the philosopher who has seen the sun).</p>\n  <p><strong>The Philosopher-King:</strong> Plato\'s most controversial idea — that only those who understand the Good should rule. Democracy, he warns, degenerates into tyranny when citizens pursue appetite over reason.</p>\n\n  <blockquote>"The measure of a man is what he does with power."</blockquote>\n\n  <div class="footer">\n    <p>Lexora Digital Library — Provided for educational purposes</p>\n  </div>\n</body>\n</html>\n',
